  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 131 AM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nThe energy from the early week rainfall deepens off the east coast \ntoday. Meanwhile, height falls ensue among the mid level ridging \noverhead as a shortwave shifts southward through Lower Michigan. \nGiven how dry we've gotten, no precipitation is expected from the \nwave. Gradient winds weaken as the low level jet regroups over the \neast coast. This allows for light winds as well. Subsidence takes \nover behind the wave and dry air continues to remain across the \narea. Given a sort of rex block to our east along the east coast, \ndry weather is expected to prevail today into early next week. As a \nvorticity streamer in the central US runs into the block, it shears \nout and dampens during the weekend into early the next work week, \nfurther indicating the strength of the block. The low low level \ntheta-e airmass slowly increases between today and early next week \nindicating that temperatures will be slowly rising after today. \nHighs in the upper 60s today, surpass 70F on Saturday and have a \n40+% chance of achieving 75F south of US-30 starting Monday.\nLows in the 40s are also expected to continue until we get to\nTuesday night. \n\nWarm advection finally begins to ensue later Tuesday into Tuesday \nnight. The greatest chance of achieving 75F is on Tuesday as a \nresult. However, this is still when medium range models differ on \nhow much moisture to bring into the area and, therefore how warm it \ngets still comes with some uncertainty. This only muddies the \nthunderstorm chance picture from that late Tuesday time into \nWednesday before chances reload for Friday and the weekend. \nTuesday's chance comes as a diffuse area of vorticity pushes \neastward in association with a cold front. The wave is rather flat \nand much better forcing is farther north. The forcing for late week \nto possibly the weekend is a cutoff low in the southwest that gets \npulled northward in the next trough. The ECMWF is notorious for\nleaving these waves in the southwest too long, but the GFS is \nnotorious for moving systems too fast. The answer may lie \nsomewhere in between.",
